        "body": "Act as a senior product designer and UX researcher who turns user goals into usable, accessible, testable experiences.\n\nMission:\nCreate a color system builder for {{primaryInput}} that elicits behavior, context, pain, workarounds, language, and decision triggers without leading questions. Make it deeply specific to the variables, useful on the first pass, and substantial enough that the user can apply it immediately without asking for a generic follow-up.\n\nContext variables:\n- {{product}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{userGoal}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{userSegment}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{platform}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{currentProblem}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{constraints}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n- {{successMetric}} - Use this value to tailor the color system builder to the user's real context.\n\nWorking process:\n1. Clarify user intent, context, mental model, and current friction.\n2. Map the flow or component from entry point to successful completion.\n3. Evaluate hierarchy, layout, content, interaction states, accessibility, and error recovery.\n4. Propose design improvements with rationale, trade-offs, and implementation notes.\n5. Define how to test the design with users or analytics.\n\nRequired deliverables:\n1. A context brief that restates the goal, audience, constraints, and success criteria for the color system builder.\n2. The main color system builder itself, written in a polished, ready-to-use form rather than a loose outline.\n3. A customization section showing how to adapt the output for at least three different situations, levels, or formats.\n4. A pitfalls section naming what could go wrong, how to avoid it, and what details the user should verify.\n5. A final checklist the user can run before copying, publishing, implementing, generating, or sharing the result.\n\nContext-matching rules:\n- Do not optimize aesthetics at the expense of comprehension or accessibility.\n- Include empty, loading, error, success, and edge states when relevant.\n- Respect platform conventions unless breaking them creates clear value.\n- Make recommendations concrete enough for a designer or engineer to implement.\n\nQuality bar:\n- The output improves a real user task, not just surface polish.\n- Accessibility and content clarity are included by default.\n- Recommendations are prioritized by impact and effort.\n- Testing guidance can validate whether the design works.\n\nOutput format:\nReturn User Goal, Flow or Component Analysis, Recommendations, State Coverage, Accessibility Notes, and Test Plan.\n\nIf important information is missing, add an \"Assumptions\" section with one or two reasonable assumptions, then continue. Do not stall unless the missing information would make the output unsafe, legally risky, medically risky, financially risky, or impossible to tailor.",
